              "text": "You can pay your U.S. federal taxes online securely and quickly. The Internal Revenue Service (IRS) offers several digital payment methods. \n\nThe best method depends on how you want to pay (bank account vs. card) and whether you are paying as an individual or a business.\n\n---\n\n### Method 1: IRS Direct Pay (Free)\n* **Best for:** Individual taxpayers who want to make a quick, one-time payment directly from a checking or savings account.\n* **Cost:** Free ($0 fees).\n* **Sign-in Required:** No.\n* **How it works:** You verify your identity using information from a previous year's tax return (such as your SSN/ITIN, filing status, and address) and input your routing and account numbers. You can use it to pay current taxes due, estimated taxes, or extension payments.\n* **Where to go:** Visit [IRS Direct Pay](https://www.irs.gov/payments/direct-pay).\n\n### Method 2: Your IRS Individual Online Account (Free)\n* **Best for:** Individuals who want to view their current tax balance, access past payment history, schedule payments in advance, or save bank details for future use.\n* **Cost:** Free ($0 fees when paying via bank account).\n* **Sign-in Required:** Yes (requires setting up or logging into an account, usually via ID.me).\n* **Where to go:** Log in or register at the [IRS Individual Online Account portal](https://www.irs.gov/payments/your-online-account).\n\n### Method 3: Debit Card, Credit Card, or Digital Wallet (Fees Apply)\n* **Best for:** Taxpayers who want to earn card rewards or pay using a digital wallet (like PayPal, Venmo, or Click to Pay).\n* **Cost:** The IRS does not keep these fees, but the third-party payment processors charge processing fees:\n  * **Debit Cards:** Usually a flat fee of around **$2.10 to $2.15** per transaction.\n  * **Credit Cards:** Typically **1.75% to 1.85%** of the total payment amount (minimum fee of $2.50).\n* **Processors:** You will pay through one of the IRS-approved processing partners (such as Pay1040 or ACI Payments, Inc.).\n* **Where to go:** View approved processors and pay via the [IRS Debit or Credit Card Payment page](https://www.irs.gov/payments/pay-your-taxes-by-debit-or-credit-card).\n\n### Method 4: Electronic Federal Tax Payment System (EFTPS) (Free)\n* **Best for:** Businesses, employers, or individuals who make large payments (over $10 million) or need to make frequent estimated payments.\n* **Cost:** Free.\n* **Requirement:** Prior enrollment is required. Once registered, you will receive a PIN in the mail to log in and schedule payments.\n* **Where to go:** Enroll or log in at the official [EFTPS Website](https://www.eftps.gov).\n\n### Method 5: Electronic Funds Withdrawal (EFW)\n* **Best for:** Taxpayers who are actively preparing and e-filing their tax return.\n* **How it works:** When you file your taxes online using software (such as TurboTax, H&R Block, Free Tax USA) or through a tax professional, you can choose to input your bank details and schedule the exact payment date directly through the filing software.\n\n---\n\n### Important Tips When Paying Online\n1. **Save Your Confirmation:** When you complete an online payment, always save or print the confirmation number. This acts as your receipt in case there is a delay in processing.\n2. **Timing:** If you make a scheduled bank payment, the funds are typically withdrawn on the date you select. Check your bank account 48 hours after the scheduled date to verify the transfer cleared.\n3. **State Taxes are Separate:** Paying federal taxes online through the IRS **does not** pay your state taxes. If you owe state income taxes, you must log onto your specific state\u2019s Department of Revenue website to make a separate payment.",
